es two opponent threats and reinforces center.\n\n- (E,7): North to D7 (.), South to F7 (.), East to E8 (.). Only west: E6 (@), E5 (@), E4 (*) — yes! So E7 would flip E6 and E5. That’s two flips. Also secures the 7th column, possibly leading to edge control.\n\n- (F,7): Similar to E7. From F7: west to F6 (.), northwest to E6 (@), then D5 (*) — diagonal path: F7 → E6 (@) → D5 (*) — only one piece flipped. Less valuable than E7 or F6.\n\n- (H,5): North to G5 (@), F5 (@), E5 (@), D5 (*) — vertical line: yes! H5 → G5 → F5 → E5 → D5 (*). So four @ pieces flipped! This is massive — flipping G5, F5, E5, and G5? Wait: H5 to D5 is vertical? No — H5 to G5 to F5 to E5 to D5 is vertical (column 5). Column 5: D5=*, E5=@, F5=@, G5=@, H5=? Yes! So placing * at H5 would sandwich all three @ pieces between H5 and D5=* — thus flipping E5, F5, G5. That’s **three** pieces! Extremely powerful move.\n\nWait — is that valid? Let's verify:\n- Column 5: D5 = *, E5 = @, F5 = @, G5 = @, H5 = empty.\n- If I place * at H5, then from H5 going north: G5 (@), F5 (@), E5 (@), D5 (*) — all @ in between, no gaps. Yes! So entire column from H5 to D5 is connected with only @ between, ends with my piece. So flipping E5, F5, G5 — **three opponent pieces** become mine. This dramatically shifts the balance.\n\nAlso, after this, my control of column 5 strengthens, and I gain presence on the H-file, aiding edge and corner strategy.\n\nCompare:\n- (H,5): flips 3 pieces — best capture.\n- (F,6): flips 2 pieces (E5, F5) via horizontal? Wait — F6 to D5 is diagonal? Let's double-check F6.\n\n(F,6): Place * at F6.\n- West: F5 (@), E5 (@), D5 (*) — yes, horizontal left. So flip F5 and E5 — 2 pieces.\n- Also, south: G6 (.), east: F7 (.), north: E6 (@), D6 (.) — no connection. Northwest: E5 (@), D6 (.) — no. So only two flipped.\n\n(E,7): Place * at E7.\n- West: E6 (@), E5 (@), E4 (*) — vertical column 7? No — same row, different columns. E7 is row E, column 7. E4 is row E, column 4. So horizontally: E4=*, E5=@, E6=@